\uc1\pard\cf1\ulnone\f0\fs20 OR058: High-Performance Dynamic Pages with Templates, XML, and mod_perl\par
Day: Fri \par
Time: 3:45pm \par
Session chair: None assigned \par
Duration: 90 minutes \par
Style: Presentation \par
Categories: Performance, Perl, XML \par
Speaker: Sander van Zoest \par
\par
\i This is first draft of notes for this ApacheCon 2000 session, for the latest\par
version of these notes please visit http://www.vanZoest.com/sander/apachecon/2000/\par
or e-mail sander@mp3.com\i0\par
\par
\ul Introduction\ulnone :\par
\par
In every group project you would like to split all the tasks that are \par
associated with the project up between the members of your team and have \par
each person be responsible for the task that most fits their experience \par
and/or motivation of the task at hand. Although this is also desired \par
producing a website, it has always been hard to split up the tasks involved\par
in developing a web site, since the content, style, user interface and \par
character sets, delivery and navigational elements are all tightly integrated.\par
\par
The objective of this session is to provide you with ideas and ways to\par
combine tools to seperate all these elements as much as possible so each\par
of these can be developed simultaneously with minimal dependancies between\par
the groups.\par
\par
When working on a large website usually you have different groups that\par
handle different parts of the website. A rough idea of what the break up\par
could be is as follows:\par
\par
o A Design Group\par
  That does layout and graphics and navigational elements to \par
  accomidate each browser. \par
o A Content group\par
  That generate and provide the text and content. \par
o An Internationalization group \par
  That does translation of all the navigational elements and \par
  content into foreign languages. \par
o A team of programmers \par
  That work on massaging the content for handling the dynamic changes \par
  in content and potentially providing the means to the design crew \par
  to provide dynamic view to the end user.\par
o And a Network and System Administration group \par
  That does all the network and system administrative tasks, such \par
  as load balancing, network architecture, switching and system\par
  configuration.\par
\par
The Network and System Administration group can be split up without too\par
much trouble when you have a working CM System and a documented process\par
on how to build and install each type of machine. The group of programmers\par
can then make specific installation scripts for each type of system. Things\par
such as Domain Name Service, Load Balancing and Monitor can then be managed\par
by the Administrative group without intervention of any other group. Since\par
our topic does not cover any of the above tasks, we will skip over this and\par
assume you have a good enough idea on how that would be split up.\par
\par
\ul How Did we get here\ulnone\par
\par
Now a little bit of history that explains to how we got where we are today.\par
After the early days of the web, people wanted a consistant style throughout\par
the website to signify a particular look and feel. This was usually \par
accomplished with static page generators or Server Side Includes that would\par
include a standard header and footer file. \par
\par
With the influx of e-commerce, bringing a catalog of merchandise onto \par
the web, provided a site for people to shop without ever leaving their \par
home. Most catalogs were maintained and stored in easy to manipulate \par
SQL compliant databases. The e-commerce wave provided the need for \par
dynamic user tracking of potential purchases and to provide an easy \par
method to browse the sites catalog that would lead to a virtual \par
check out line.\par
\par
Most of these online catalogs were either dynamicly generated by custom\par
CGI scripts written specificly for each site using C, perl, java.\par
Development with these tools, usually meant that people would embed\par
stylistic elements within the source code, which made it hard for\par
the design team to make changes on dynamicly generated content without\par
bugging the engineering team.\par
\par
Specificly designed development tools such as Cold Fusion, Microsoft\par
Active Server Pages (ASP) and PHP/FI made this a little easier, but\par
did not provide the flexability has the more programmer specific \par
languages did, which had access to a wealth of packages already \par
written to do just about anything you ever dreamed of doing. Also\par
the learning curve on these tools was usually a higher, because they\par
had their own specific syntax.\par
\par
The specificly designed development tools usually were logical extensions\par
to the static page generators and the Server Side Include modules and\par
were built into the web server. The more people found out about the\par
web the more overloaded the servers became. The specificly designed\par
development tools usually out performed the custom CGI scripts, because\par
the CGI gateway fork() and exec()'d the custom scripts for each \par
request. This limitation brought about server modules such as \par
mod_fastcgi, mod_jserv and mod_perl which each in their own way, solved\par
the fork() and exec() performance hit and allowed developers to continue\par
development in their programming language of choice.\par
\par
Things such as co-branding and affiliate marketing, became next few\par
hot topics on the net. These new topics required the sharing of either\par
the content or style over onto other sites that were not controlled\par
by the same group of individuals or even companies. Remote Virtual\par
Server Includes (SSI), JavaScript and nightly pulls of content and\par
brands were some of the ways this was accomplished.\par
\par
\ul A Quick Overview\ulnone\par
\par
There have been a lot of tools created to help meet lots of different\par
requirements set forth in the way we use the web. Now we will combine \par
these tools and come up with a robust web publishing infrastructure \par
that is focused on just-in-time page assembly, performance and \par
scalablility and allowed to re-use as many tools already in the \par
market today.\par
\par
To create a web page visable by a browser, you need to combine content,\par
design and a particular natural language you want to publish in such\par
as English.\par
\par
To create the HTML document, you take an English Template/StyleSheet\par
and apply the English XML content. This can be done on-the-fly by\par
the apache web server, using a building stylesheet parser module and\par
URI translation module to link up the appropriate stylesheet with\par
the requested content. This method allows the Design Team to develop\par
the stylesheet template and the content by the Content Group. The\par
Internationalization Group can then take each and provide translations\par
without needing to work closely with either group.\par
\par
Perl can then be used by the Engineering Team to provide Dynamic views\par
of the XML content that will be picked up by the Template for display\par
to the End User.\par
\par
Because of the seperation of the style from the content. There is no\par
reason the Template needs to generate HTML, it could generate HDML,\par
WML for mobile handheld devices or just about any other format. Any\par
component of the request can determine which template to use and \par
which branded design to display.\par
\par
\i Note:\i0\par
\i This concept was implemented by MP3.com, Inc. using Apache, C and mod_perl\par
with performance and just-in-time assembly over feature set in mind. For \par
an implementation of a similar concept using Java and Java Servlets with \par
a full XSL implementation see The Cocoon Project http://xml.apache.org/cocoon/ \par
and Stefano Mazzocchi's Nightschool Session "Adding XML Capabilities with Cocoon".\par
\i0\par
\ul\b The Details\ulnone\b0\par
\par
\ul Content\ulnone\par
\par
As described above, XML provided us with a most standardized way to\par
manage and deliver content. Sometimes the XML simply contains pointers\par
to how to get to the content and other times if the content is embedded.\par
\par
Some of the other sessions such as Pier Paolo Fumagalli's "XML Publishing \par
Fundamentals", Philip Grabowski's "XML: An Intensive Introduction" and\par
Ted Leung's "Everything You Always Wanted to Know About XML Parsing" should\par
give you a good background and concept of XML, so we won't go into much\par
detail here.\par
\par
Generation of XML can be done in several ways. If you already have all your\par
content in a SQL Database, you can use perl's DBI and XML::Writer modules\par
to generate XML from your SQL databases that will be used.  \par
\par
\ul Style\ulnone\par
At MP3.com, Inc. we developed our own Style Template Language that behaves\par
similarily to XSL called Yet Another Style Language (YASL). \par
There is no real reason we can not use XSL or a similar technology\par
here, but at development time there was no C-based XSL implementation\par
that suited our needs. We required just-in-time generation, performance\par
and scalability.\par
\par
For more information about XSL visit http://www.w3.org/ or the sessions \par
"XML Publishing Fundamentals" by Pier Paolo Fumagalli and "Practical XSLT \par
Transformations for Fun and Profit" by Scott Boag.\par
\par
We have been looking at the XML Apache Projects for future possible \par
integration so we can use Xerces as our XML parser and to use the more \par
full featured stylesheet language as provided by Xalan instead. I have not had\par
too much time lately to devote to Apache XML projects, but definately am\par
interested in helping the development effort and I am looking forward to\par
implement a similar solution using these open source tools so that everyone\par
can benefit from what we have learned.\par
\par
