Notice the __DEFAULT__ value used for the <yasl:xml> tag, this\par
tells YASL to use path information from the URI to find the appropriate\par
XML data file and with regex match configuration directive defined in\par
the directory it knows which template to use. This technique allows you\par
to have a single style template for an unlimited amount of XML content.\par
\par
\ul Mod_Perl\ulnone\par
\par
How does mod_perl interact with the Style Template?\par
\par
For more information about mod_perl visit http://perl.apache.org/ or the\par
sessions "Getting Started with Mod Perl (Part I & II)" and "Improving\par
Script Performance Under mod_perl" by Stas Bekman.\par
\par
To generate XML content on the fly we use the perl scripting language\par
using the mod_perl apache module. To talk to the Yasl Stylesheet Language\par
and XML parser Expat-lite (Build into Apache since version 1.3.9), we\par
use an internal redirect to the Yasl template to have the generated XML \par
be displayed in the browser.\par
\par
To provide Yasl with access to the dynamicly generated XML, we use a\par
modified version of XML::Writer that uses XS glue to talk to Expat-lite\par
and mod_yasl. \par
\par
The constructor of this version of XML::Writer makes a call to mod_yasl \par
to allocate a pool of memory to be used by Expat to store the XML content\par
and returns an integer to perl that it can use to reference this new \par
pool of memory created.\par
\par
With this new version of XML::Writer the developer can make the usual\par
calls to XML::Writer to generate XML content except that it will now\par
be stored in data structure in the memory pool allocated by mod_yasl.\par
\par
After the developer has written out the appropriate XML content. The\par
developer will put the integer to reference the memory pool in the\par
notes field of the request. This tells Yasl which memory pool to use\par
for the XML content after the perl code makes an internal redirect\par
to the appropriate Yasl template.\par
\par
\f1 # This mod_perl module implements an example content handler for\par
# ApacheCon 2000 - http://www.ApacheCon.com/\par
#\par
# It operates within the New Publishing Model, so it generates xml\par
# and lets yasl do the actual rendering.\par
\par
package ApacheCon::Example;\par
\par
use strict;\par
use lib qw(/mp3/tools/yasl /mp3/tools/MP3Com/lib);\par
use CGI;\par
use Apache::Constants qw(:common);\par
use Apache::Log;\par
use XMLapi::XMLWriter;\par
use MP3Com::XMLWriter;\par
\par
sub handler \par
\{\par
  my $r = shift;\par
\par
  # if the url we're called from has xml_raw in it, show the xml as text/plain\par
  my $dir_config = $r->dir_config();\par
  my $raw_xml = $dir_config->\{RawXMLMode\};\par
  my $template;\par
  my $xw ;  #xmlwriter object.\par
  if (!$raw_xml) \{\par
    $xw = XMLapi::XMLWriter->new();\par
    if (!defined $xw) \{\par
      $r->log->crit("Can't allocate an XML handle.");\par
      return SERVER_ERROR;\par
    \}\par
  \} else \{\par
    $xw = MP3Com::XMLWriter->new(\\*STDOUT);\par
  \}\par
\par
  my $cgi = new CGI;\par
  $template = $dir_config->\{TemplateDir\} . $cgi->path_info();\par
  if (!defined $template) \{\par
    $r->log->crit("No template configured.\\n");\par
    return SERVER_ERROR;\par
  \}\par
  if ($raw_xml) \{\par
    $r->content_type("text/plain");\par
    $r->send_http_header();\par
  \}\par
  $xw->starttag("Yasl");\par
  # write the page xml data to the xml handle.\par
   $xw->element("date", scalar( localtime ) );\par
   $xw->element("title","Dynamic XML Demo: Pid " . $$);\par
   foreach (sort $cgi->param) \{\par
      $xw->starttag("Params");\par
      $xw->element("name",  $_);\par
      $xw->element("value", $cgi->param($_));\par
      $xw->endtag();\par
   \}\par
  $xw->endtag();\par
  return OK if ($raw_xml);\par
  $r->notes("XML_HANDLE", $xw->handle());\par
  $r->internal_redirect($template);\par
  return OK;\par
\}\par
\par
1;\par
