Gotchas and Problems.\par
---------------------\par
- One of the problems with converting XML to HTML is that it uses a\par
  similar set of entities, such as the &amp;, that need to be encoded\par
  when putting content in XML and HTML.\par
   \par
  When the content is requested by mod_yasl it does not know if the\par
  expected output should be an &amp; or simply just an &.\par
\par
  You would think that you could just put it in the XML the way you\par
  would like to see it on the HTML page, but what if you wanted to\par
  convert the same XML document to PostScript instead?\par
\par
  To solve this problem we added some HTML entity encoding arguments\par
  to <yasl:value>, so you that the templates can decide if they want\par
  the entity or the actual value.\par
\par
- A similar issue is that of quotation marks. Because the XML content\par
  is not known ahead of time putting a yasl:value inside a form input\par
  field could be problematic. Consider the following Yasl template \par
  segment.\f1\par
\par
   <INPUT TYPE="TEXT" VALUE="<yasl:value "Message"/>">\f0\par
\par
  If the XML contents of Message did not contain a double quote \par
  everything would work without any problems. Now if it was a\par
  double quote the browser would have a fit.\par
\par
  This situation does not come up very often and since it usually\par
  does not come up unless we use FORM tags which are only useful\par
  in HTML; we currently just solve this by adding another argument\par
  to <yasl:value> that will escape any single and double quote to\par
  the appropriate HTML entities.\par
\par
- As shown by the recent CERT advisory URI and Entity encoding is\par
  very important for security reasons. It also can be someone confusing\par
  in template work since you are never really sure if the text is\par
  going to be used in an anchor tag or simply is a piece of text\par
  that needs to be displayed. \par
\par
  In the case of an anchor and image tag you would like to see URI \par
  encoding while just about any other time you would expect HTML \par
  entity encoding. This is another option we added to <yasl:value> \par
  that can tell yasl to use URI encoding instead of the default HTML\par
  entity encoding.\par
\par
  This could probably have been determined programmaticly by having\par
  yasl pay more attention to the template it is working with, but\par
  we would rather specify this ourselves then having the performance\par
  overhead of yasl keeping track of this scenario and handle it \par
  appropriately.\par
\par
- Navigational Elements in content XML seems like something that\par
  would come up every so often for pages that have navigational\par
  elements that depend on the amount of content and which content\par
  is currently being displayed.\par
\par
  This is somewhat of a bummer, because this clutters the clear line\par
  between content and design elements and layout. I would probably\par
  suggest using a second, but smaller XML file that would dictate\par
  these kind of things. Especially because the real estate available\par
  differs dramaticly if you are on a WAP capable phone, Web browser\par
  or on a Legal size piece of paper.\par
.\par
- Performance, in some cases parsing XML is too much overhead and\par
  generated DBM files instead (or from the XML) solves much of this\par
  problem.\par
